# Admin console — platform staff, always signed in
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. `.env.local` is gitignored; this template
# is tracked on purpose so a fresh clone can see the contract.
#
# Every variable here is validated by src/lib/env.schema.ts. A malformed value
# fails `next build` with one line per problem rather than throwing on some
# later request — so if the build is complaining about the environment, this
# file is the thing to fix.
#
# Two phases, because a build and a Worker do not see the same environment:
#
#   next.config.ts  → parseBuildEnv()  at BUILD time
#   src/lib/env.ts  → parseBuildEnv()  eagerly, + accessors validated on use
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is required at BUILD time — Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_*
# into the bundle, so a missing value is baked in permanently and no runtime
# configuration recovers it. In CI it must be a build environment variable.
#
# SESSION_SECRET is NOT required at build time. On Cloudflare it arrives at
# request time as a `wrangler secret`, so a build container legitimately does
# not have it. Set locally it is still fully validated.

# Origin of rimalis-api. NO trailing slash, no path.
# Local: the API defaults to port 4000 (see api/.env.example).
#
# ⚠️ The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is inherited from the scaffold and is MISLEADING.
# Under the BFF (ADR-0003) the browser never calls this origin: it calls this
# app's own route handlers and Server Actions, which call the API
# server-to-server. That matters more here than on the storefront — every vendor
# call is authenticated, so a browser-side fetch would need the access token in
# the browser, which is the one thing the BFF exists to prevent.
# `src/lib/env.ts` is `server-only` specifically so that mistake is a build error.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Path prefix every API route mounts under. MUST match BASE_PATH on the API —
# it is a variable rather than a constant so the two cannot drift.
API_BASE_PATH=/api/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Under the BFF this app's route handlers hold the session cookie, so the secret
# that encrypts it belongs here, NOT on the API.
#
# The cookie is ENCRYPTED, not merely signed — JWE `dir` + A256GCM via `jose`.
# `httpOnly` stops JavaScript reading it; it does not stop a TLS-terminating
# proxy log, a browser profile on disk, or a `Set-Cookie` echoed into an
# observability trace (and wrangler.jsonc has `observability.enabled`). So the
# access and refresh tokens inside stay unreadable rather than merely
# untampered-with.
#
# A256GCM needs exactly 32 bytes. Generate one — do not invent a passphrase,
# which will fail the byte-length check:
#
#     openssl rand -base64url 32
#
# ...or, on an openssl too old for -base64url:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
#
# ⚠️ Use a THIRD secret, different from BOTH the marketplace's and the vendor
# app's. Sharing one would let a cookie minted by one app decrypt in another,
# which is exactly the cross-app session bleed that three separate subdomains
# exist to prevent — and it matters most here, because this is the session that
# grants ADMIN.
#
# ⚠️ The cookie NAME also differs per app (`rimalis_admin_session` here) and
# that is load-bearing in development: all three apps are `localhost`, and
# cookies ignore the port, so a shared name means signing into one app silently
# signs you out of another. See the header of src/lib/session.ts.
SESSION_SECRET=
